Why this file exists, and what it is not
----------------------------------------

It is not a licence and it grants nothing. It is the statement of the offer, for
the two channels that cannot spell the offer in their own metadata.

npm rejects a LicenseRef expression outright -- measured with
validate-npm-package-license 3.0.4, the library npm itself uses -- and
nuget.org refuses one with NU5124. Both offer the same way out: point at a file.
Until 07-09-2026 that pointer resolved to LICENSE, because LICENSE then opened
with this same statement. Since then LICENSE holds the AGPL text and nothing
else, so that a licence scanner reading it sees the AGPL rather than prose it
cannot classify -- which is the whole point of that change, and it is why the
pointer needs a file of its own rather than a sentence bolted onto the licence.

The full explanation, with the history and what the build currently does, is the
Licence section of README.md.
